The core moves, applied to all four tabs:

Charcoal instead of default grey. #15130f/#1c1912 reads as "app" not "terminal window." Terminal-ness mostly comes from pure black-on-grey with zero elevation.
One accent colour, used sparingly. Amber/rust (#e8a33d) for the active tab, favourites, and the Connect button — nothing else competes with it.
Status as colour, not just text. A left-edge stripe (green = online, red = offline) lets you scan the list without reading "ONLINE"/"OFFLINE" every row. Ping can do the same — green under ~50ms, amber/red climbing.
Rows, not spreadsheet cells. Grouping name + map + IP + version into one dense two-line block per server cuts visual noise way down versus 10 separate flat columns, and gives the server name room to breathe instead of truncating mid-word.
Muted secondary text. IP, port, version, "last updated" all drop to a dimmer grey — only the things you act on (name, player count, Connect) stay bright.
Consistent radius and 1px hairline borders instead of the sharp OS-default table borders — that alone kills a lot of the terminal feel.

For the Server Browser (the 790-row table), I'd keep it as an actual dense table — that one should stay data-dense — but apply the same dark palette, muted secondary columns (host/query port/last checked), and colour only the Status column. The Mods tab could get simple green check icons instead of ✓ Installed text, and Settings could group its 17 checks into 3-4 collapsible sections (Launch, Steam, Paths, Diagnostics) rather than one long OK-list.

QSS = """
QWidget {
    background-color: #15130f;
    color: #efe9db;
    font-size: 13px;
}

/* Tab bar */
QTabWidget::pane { border: none; background: #15130f; }
QTabBar::tab {
    background: #1c1912;
    color: #8a8477;
    padding: 10px 18px;
    border: none;
    border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
}
QTabBar::tab:selected {
    color: #e8a33d;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #e8a33d;
}
QTabBar::tab:hover:!selected { color: #c9c2af; }

/* Tables */
QTableWidget, QTableView {
    background: #1c1912;
    alternate-background-color: #201c15;
    gridline-color: #2a251c;
    border: 1px solid #322c20;
    border-radius: 8px;
    selection-background-color: #3a2f1a;
    selection-color: #efe9db;
}
QHeaderView::section {
    background: #1c1912;
    color: #8a8477;
    padding: 8px;
    border: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #322c20;
    font-weight: 500;
}
QTableWidget::item { padding: 6px; border: none; }
QTableWidget::item:selected { background: #3a2f1a; }

/* Buttons */
QPushButton {
    background: transparent;
    color: #e8a33d;
    border: 1px solid #4a3f2a;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 8px 16px;
}
QPushButton:hover { background: #2a2214; }
QPushButton:pressed { background: #201a10; }
QPushButton:disabled { color: #5f5a4d; border-color: #322c20; }

/* Primary action button — give it an objectName e.g. "primaryBtn" */
QPushButton#primaryBtn {
    background: #e8a33d;
    color: #241a08;
    border: none;
    font-weight: 600;
}
QPushButton#primaryBtn:hover { background: #f0b158; }

/* Inputs */
QLineEdit, QComboBox, QSpinBox {
    background: #201c15;
    border: 1px solid #322c20;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 6px 10px;
    color: #efe9db;
}
QLineEdit:focus, QComboBox:focus { border: 1px solid #e8a33d; }

QScrollBar:vertical { background: #15130f; width: 10px; }
QScrollBar::handle:vertical { background: #322c20; border-radius: 5px; min-height: 24px; }
QScrollBar::handle:vertical:hover { background: #4a3f2a; }
"""
app.setStyleSheet(QSS)

Status/ping colouring can't come from QSS alone — you'll want to set item.setForeground(QColor("#6fcf97")) (or a custom QStyledItemDelegate) per-cell when populating the ONLINE/OFFLINE and ping columns, since QSS can't do conditional colour based on cell content.
For the left-edge status stripe on the Servers tab, that's easier to fake as a small coloured square/dot glyph in the first column (item.setIcon(...) with a solid-colour pixmap) than a real CSS border-left, since QTableWidget rows don't support per-row left borders cleanly.
QHeaderView::section styling only kicks in if horizontalHeader().setStyleSheet() isn't overriding it elsewhere — worth checking if you've set anything on the header directly.
If you want the amber focus ring on inputs, QLineEdit:focus works but QComboBox:focus sometimes needs QComboBox:on too depending on your Qt version.
